# PRODUCT REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT (PRD)
## Gold Covering Jewellery Brand Website

**Document version:** 1.0  
**Project type:** Customer-facing brand website  
**Working brand name:** Uma Fashions (confirm with the client before launch)  
**Status:** Requirements baseline — obtain client approval before building

---

## 1. Purpose

Create an elegant, responsive website for a business selling gold-covering/gold-plated jewellery and ladies' accessories. The website introduces the brand, showcases verified collections, builds trust, and directs visitors to contact the store or continue to the shopping web application.

This document defines product requirements and implementation/deployment expectations. It is a PRD, not a substitute for testing or client approval.

## 2. Goals and success criteria

- Present a premium, artistic visual identity using a restrained gold palette.
- Make the business, product categories, contact information, and policies easy to find.
- Work smoothly on mobile, tablet, and desktop.
- Load images efficiently and provide accessible navigation.
- Give the owner a simple, documented process to update content.
- Deploy to a live HTTPS URL and hand over ownership and access safely.

**Success checks:** all agreed pages are complete; no placeholder text remains; client approves content; links and mobile navigation work; production build passes; site is live over HTTPS; accessibility and performance basics are reviewed.

## 3. Users

- **Potential customer:** explores collections, learns about the business, and contacts the store.
- **Returning customer:** revisits products and uses contact/social links.
- **Store owner:** confirms the brand story, product images, policies, and business information.
- **Developer/content editor:** maintains code and updates approved content.

## 4. Scope

### In scope
- Responsive home page
- Collections/category showcase
- About/story section or page
- Gallery/lookbook
- Contact page/section
- Approved policy pages/links
- Footer with verified store and owner information
- Search-engine metadata, favicon, sitemap, and robots rules
- Subtle scroll/hover animations with reduced-motion support
- Git-based source control and deployment

### Out of scope unless separately approved
- Customer accounts, shopping cart, checkout, payment processing, order tracking
- Inventory/admin dashboard
- Automated emails, SMS, loyalty points, reviews, back-in-stock alerts
- Custom domain purchase, paid photography, paid hosting, paid third-party services

These commerce features belong in the separate **Gold Covering Jewellery Web Application PRD**.

## 5. Brand and design requirements

- **Visual direction:** luxury, warm, editorial, artistic, uncluttered.
- **Palette:** warm ivory, espresso/brown, muted gold, champagne highlights, accessible neutral text.
- **Fonts:** Cormorant Garamond for headings; Jost for body text, navigation, and buttons.
- **Motion:** restrained liquid-gold shimmer, smooth section reveals, gentle parallax where appropriate, and polished hover states. Avoid motion that blocks reading or harms performance.
- **Accessibility:** respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; keyboard-operable navigation; visible focus; descriptive alt text; sufficient contrast; form labels.
- **Photography:** use client-approved product images with consistent lighting and accurate colour. Compress images and avoid layout shift.

## 6. Information architecture and requirements

### 6.1 Home
- Brand header and responsive navigation.
- Hero image, concise brand statement, and primary call to action.
- Featured collections.
- Featured products or lookbook cards, based only on real stock/content.
- Brand story and approved trust information.
- Contact call to action and complete footer.

**Acceptance criteria:** primary CTA works; all featured cards link to valid destinations; layout adapts to narrow screens; no unapproved product claims.

### 6.2 Collections
- Show only categories actually sold by the business.
- Candidate categories may include earrings, necklaces, chains, bangles, jewellery sets, and ladies' accessories; confirm the final list with the owner.
- Each card has an image, accessible text, and working destination.

**Acceptance criteria:** no dead links or misleading category names; images have alt text.

### 6.3 About
- Approved business story, location context, and approach to product selection.
- Do not invent years in business, craftsmanship claims, certifications, or customer numbers.

**Acceptance criteria:** all factual claims are approved by the client.

### 6.4 Gallery/lookbook
- Curated product/lifestyle images.
- Optimized responsive image sizes.
- Image permissions confirmed.

### 6.5 Contact
- Verified phone number, address, business hours, map link, and approved social/WhatsApp links.
- Use click-to-call links on mobile where suitable.

**Acceptance criteria:** every contact link is tested; do not expose personal details without owner approval.

### 6.6 Policies
- Link to client-approved privacy, shipping, return/exchange, and terms information.
- Do not write unsupported legal guarantees or publish unreviewed tax statements.

### 6.7 Footer
- Verified store name and owner details, if approved for publication.
- Address, phone, hours, social links, and policy links.
- Copyright notice.

**Acceptance criteria:** no `[BRACKET]` placeholders remain; owner explicitly approves public display of their details.

## 7. Content accuracy and compliance requirements

- Describe products accurately as **gold covering** or **gold-plated**, as applicable.
- Do not claim “pure gold,” solid gold, purity, hallmarking, or certification unless accurate and independently documented.
- Use only authorized logos, images, testimonials, and customer content.
- All prices, product descriptions, contact information, business identifiers, and policies must be supplied or verified by the owner.
- Any GSTIN or other legal identifier must be provided and checked by the business owner; never fabricate one.

## 8. Technical requirements

Recommended implementation:
- Next.js 14 with TypeScript and App Router, subject to compatibility checks.
- Tailwind CSS and accessible reusable UI components.
- CSS transitions for simple motion; optional motion library only if needed.
- Next.js image optimization where supported.
- Git and GitHub for version control.
- Vercel or Netlify as candidate hosting providers; check current plan terms and framework support before choosing.

### Non-functional requirements
- Responsive from small phones to desktop.
- Semantic HTML, keyboard navigation, alt text, focus states, reduced-motion support.
- Unique page titles and descriptions.
- Optimized images and minimal unnecessary JavaScript.
- No secrets, private documents, or customer data in public assets or Git.
- Clear README with install, run, build, and deploy instructions.

## 9. Build workflow

1. Get written approval for the plan, scope, brand, content, images, and policies.
2. Confirm business name, owner details, address, phone, categories, hours, and URLs.
3. Install supported Node.js LTS, Git, and VS Code on Windows.
4. Create the Next.js project and run it locally.
5. Implement shared header/footer and design tokens.
6. Build the home, collections, about, gallery, contact, and policy routes.
7. Add responsive styling, motion, metadata, and optimized imagery.
8. Run lint and production build; test all routes and devices.
9. Push source to GitHub without secrets.
10. Deploy a preview and obtain client approval.
11. Deploy production, verify HTTPS/domain and all links, and hand over access.

Typical commands (run in PowerShell after installing Node.js and Git):

```powershell
npx create-next-app@14 uma-fashions-website
cd uma-fashions-website
npm run dev
npm run lint
npm run build
```

Use the CLI prompts to select TypeScript, Tailwind, App Router, ESLint, and the recommended import alias. Open `http://localhost:3000` for local development.

## 10. Free/low-cost deployment requirements

Potential options to evaluate:
- **Vercel:** convenient for Next.js; verify current Hobby eligibility, commercial-use terms, usage quotas, and features.
- **Netlify:** alternative hosting; verify current Next.js adapter/feature compatibility and credit limits.
- **Render Static Sites or GitHub Pages:** only suitable if the site is built as a compatible static export; not a replacement for server features.

Official references:
- Next.js deployment: https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/deploying
- Vercel pricing: https://vercel.com/pricing
- Netlify pricing: https://www.netlify.com/pricing/
- Render free services: https://render.com/docs/free
- GitHub: https://github.com/

Free tiers can change and may restrict commercial use or impose usage limits. A custom domain usually costs money. Confirm current terms before promising free production hosting to the client.

## 11. Testing and acceptance

- Test every route and all internal/external links.
- Test navigation and layout on phone, tablet, and desktop.
- Test keyboard navigation, focus visibility, contrast, image alt text, and reduced motion.
- Check page titles, descriptions, favicon, sitemap, and robots rules.
- Run `npm run lint` and `npm run build`.
- Check image sizes, loading behaviour, and horizontal overflow.
- Confirm no placeholder copy, broken images, console errors, or unsupported claims.
- Obtain written client approval of the live version.

## 12. Deployment and handover

- Create a GitHub repository and push only reviewed source code.
- Import the repository into the selected host and use the detected framework defaults unless there is a documented reason to change them.
- Test the deployment URL before adding a custom domain.
- Configure DNS using only the records supplied by the hosting provider.
- Verify HTTPS, canonical domain, redirects, and live links.
- Keep the client as owner/admin of domain, repository, and hosting accounts where possible; use MFA and least-privilege access.
- Provide a short README describing how to run locally, update content, deploy changes, and report issues.
- Agree on maintenance responsibility and whether a paid tier will be required as traffic grows.

## 13. Risks and mitigations

- **Incorrect business details:** require owner verification before publishing.
- **Misleading product claims:** use accurate gold-covering/gold-plated descriptions and obtain approval.
- **Free-tier changes:** verify terms before launch; document migration options.
- **Slow images/animations:** optimize assets, reduce motion, and test on mobile.
- **Account ownership confusion:** keep client ownership clear and document access.
- **Scope creep:** keep commerce functionality in the separate application project unless added through an approved change.

## 14. Open questions before build

- Final public store name and logo?
- Which product categories are actually sold?
- Which owner details may be published?
- Verified address, phone, hours, and social URLs?
- Which policy text has the owner approved?
- Is this website informational only, or should it link to the shopping application?
- Does the client own a domain already?
- Which host's current free-tier terms are acceptable?

## 15. Sign-off checklist

- [ ] Scope and design approved by client.
- [ ] Product categories, imagery, and claims approved.
- [ ] Contact and owner details verified.
- [ ] Policy content approved.
- [ ] All placeholders removed.
- [ ] Lint and production build pass.
- [ ] Responsive and accessibility checks completed.
- [ ] Deployment and HTTPS verified.
- [ ] Hosting/domain ownership and handover agreed.
- [ ] Client approved the live URL.
